# AIsense local policy — subjects and rules for traffic seen on THIS device.
#
# Copyright (C) 2026 Optim Enterprises BV. BSD 3-Clause.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
#
# The reputation feed answers "what should every device block". This file
# answers "what should THIS firewall block, for WHICH client" — the per-subject
# rules that make up a parental-control or per-segment policy.
#
# IT SHIPS EMPTY ON PURPOSE, and that is a safety property rather than an
# unfinished one: an empty policy blocks nothing. Turning local blocking on
# without writing a policy here would attach a live interface, run the whole
# capture pipeline, and enforce nothing — which reads as "configured" in every
# log while doing no work. Write a policy first, then enable capture.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUBJECTS
#
# A subject is a client, identified by MAC. Rules attach to a subject, so
# traffic from an unknown client matches no rule and is never blocked.
#
#   subject <mac> <label>
#
# IMPORTANT, AND THIS IS THE LIMIT THAT MATTERS ON A FIREWALL:
#   These MACs must be the client's OWN address. On a ROUTED firewall a
#   forwarded packet carries the ROUTER's source MAC, not the client's, so
#   this file cannot match LAN clients on OPNsense the way it does on an
#   OpenWrt AP. Until a routed-client identity source exists, local rules
#   apply to traffic originated BY this device.
#
# subject 02:00:00:00:00:01 kid-tablet
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RULES
#
#   block <mac|label> app <tag>          block an application for a subject
#   allow <mac|label> app <tag>          allow it (overrides a block)
#
# Tags come from the signature database (share/aether-sensord/appdb.cfg). A tag
# that names no application is REFUSED at load time and counted, so a typo is a
# visible error rather than a rule that quietly never fires.
#
# block kid-tablet app youtube
# block kid-tablet app tiktok
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO TURN THIS ON, in order
#
#   1. Write at least one subject and rule above.
#   2. Check the config parses, before touching the datapath:
#        aether-sensord-pf -c /usr/local/etc/aether-sensord.conf -t
#   3. Register the table as an OPNsense static alias, or nothing enforces.
#   4. Start in OBSERVE mode first (capture_enforce = no). The whole pipeline
#      runs and reports what it WOULD block, and writes nothing. Read the
#      counters:
#        local: available=1 flows=N decided=N applied=0 ...
#      `decided` > 0 with `applied` = 0 is observe mode working as intended.
#   5. Only then set capture_enforce = yes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT STILL WILL NOT DO
#
# No DNS resolution happens anywhere in this path, so a blocked destination is
# denied from the SECOND connection onward, not the first. The address cannot be
# known before the client's own handshake reveals it. This is advisory
# enforcement, not a filter that stops browsing on the first attempt.
